the formatting engine
=====================

stdoc_printf and stdoc_fprintf are thin wrappers: they start a
va_list, call stdoc_vfprintf, and then either discard what the call
had staged (on failure, so that half of a conversion never reaches
the output) or flush it (on success, because the freestanding startup
path installs no exit handler and a buffer left dirty would never be
written).  Everything else happens in io/vfprintf.cxx.

The wrappers and the engine are C++23, but every function the headers
declare is defined with C linkage, so the archive exports the same
unmangled names it always did; the internal helpers of the engine live
in an anonymous namespace.


How one call flows
------------------

    stdoc_printf / stdoc_fprintf
        |
        v
    stdoc_vfprintf(stream, format, args)
        |
        +-- borrow the scratch block from the heap (see below)
        |
        +-- parser: flags -> width -> precision -> length -> conversion
        |         a run of ordinary characters is emitted as it is
        |
        +-- output_write / output_repeat / output_field / output_number
        |         |
        |         v
        +-- stdoc_stream_write (io/file.cxx) -> staging buffer -> write(2)

Parser and formatter are kept in one translation unit on purpose: the
specification has to be fully known before any byte is emitted, and
splitting parsing from emission would force the whole specification to
be marshalled through a second interface.

The parser and the formatter are stateless with respect to each
conversion; the only state a call carries is the stream, the running
character count and a sticky failure flag.  The first failure stops
the whole call without further system calls.


The dialect
-----------

Conversions:

    %d %i          signed decimal int
    %u             unsigned decimal int
    %o             unsigned octal int
    %x %X          unsigned hexadecimal int (lower/upper case)
    %c             int converted to unsigned char
    %s             null-terminated string
    %p             pointer (a null pointer prints as "(nil)")
    %%             a literal percent sign

Fields:

    flags          '-' left align, '+' force sign, ' ' space sign,
                   '#' alternative form, '0' zero pad - in any order
                   and any number of repetitions
    width          '*' from an argument, or decimal digits
    precision      '.' followed by '*' or digits; a bare '.' means 0
    length         hh, h, l, ll, z, t, j

Details that are easy to get wrong and are covered by the tests:

  * A negative '*' width is the '-' flag with a positive width;
    INT_MIN saturates rather than overflowing on negation.
  * A negative '*' precision is treated as if no precision were given.
  * A null "%s" argument prints as "(null)", or as nothing at all when
    the precision is narrower than that word.
  * The layout follows the standard: optional sign, optional prefix
    ("0x", or a leading "0" for octal), the zeros demanded by the
    precision, the digits, then the field padding.  Zero padding from
    the '0' flag is merged with the precision zeros and placed after
    the sign, which is what makes "%-8.4d" and "%08.4d" differ only in
    alignment.
  * The '+' and ' ' flags apply to the signed conversions and to %p.
    On a non-null pointer they are printed before the "0x" prefix -
    "%+p" of 0x1234 prints "+0x1234" - which is what the reference C
    library does; a null pointer prints "(nil)" with no sign.
  * Flags that do not apply to a conversion are ignored: "%+u" and
    "% u" print the value unchanged, and "%#u" prints no prefix.

Not supported in this release: floating-point conversions, positional
arguments ("%1$s"), the "%n" conversion and the "I" / "'" flags.  An
unrecognised conversion consumes its argument and emits nothing, which
is undefined behaviour by the standard; emitting nothing is the least
surprising choice here.


Checking the format string
--------------------------

stdoc_printf and stdoc_fprintf are declared with the GNU
"format(printf)" attribute when the compiler understands it, so a call
whose arguments do not match its format string is reported while the
program is built rather than when it runs.  The attribute is the only
compiler extension in the public header: it changes no declaration and
no calling convention, both GNU C and Clang provide it, and it expands
to nothing anywhere else, so the header is still ISO C17.

The check is the compiler's and it is a little stricter than this
interface in one place.  "%+p" and "% p" are accepted here, following
the reference C library, but "-Wformat" reports the '+' and ' ' flags on
a pointer conversion, because the C standard leaves them undefined
there.  A caller that wants that output, knowing what it prints, can
quiet the one diagnostic around the one call:

    #pragma GCC diagnostic push
    #pragma GCC diagnostic ignored "-Wformat"
    stdoc_printf("%+p\n", pointer);
    #pragma GCC diagnostic pop

test/format.c does exactly that around the cases that pin the sign flags
on a pointer down.


Refusing to produce output
--------------------------

The return value is the number of characters written, or -1.  Three
things make a call fail:

  * A width or precision written as a literal that does not fit in an
    "int" - "%9999999999d" is a failure, not a very wide field.  The
    accumulator is clamped internally so a long run of digits cannot
    wrap it.
  * A field that would not fit in the return value.  The check happens
    before a single byte of the field is written: a width of INT_MAX
    would otherwise push gigabytes at the kernel and only then fail.
  * A write the operating system rejects, which also becomes a sticky
    error on the stream: every later call fails immediately without
    touching the system-call layer.

Digits are rendered backwards into a scratch buffer from the end, so
the storage is never read past the digits into whatever the previous
call left there.  Division and modulus by 8, 10 and 16 are compiled to
ordinary arithmetic on both architectures this library implements, so
no compiler runtime helper is required - which matters because the
library links with -nostdlib.


Scratch memory
--------------

Every call borrows one block of 97 bytes from the libstdoc heap: the
first 65 bytes are the digit storage of output_number, the remaining
32 bytes are the padding chunk of output_repeat.  The two regions
never overlap, and the block is released on every path out of
stdoc_vfprintf, including the two failure paths of the parser, so a
completed call leaves nothing allocated.

When the heap has no block to spare, the helpers fall back to stack
buffers of the same size: an exhausted heap costs stack space rather
than a failed conversion.  The formatted functions therefore never
fail because of the heap.  See docs/heap.


Testing
-------

test/format.c prints the output of every supported conversion, flag,
field and length modifier together with the value each call reports.
Its golden file was produced by a reference C library, so the test
checks the dialect rather than the implementation's habits.  See
docs/testing for how to regenerate it.

test/differential.c covers the same dialect more widely and against a
live reference: the same source is compiled once against the host C
library and once against libstdoc, and the two are compared case by
case.  Its cases are built at run time from every combination of flags,
width and precision, so it reaches combinations nobody would write by
hand - a precision on %c, the '#' flag on %d - which is where two
implementations are most likely to disagree.

test/errors.c covers the failure paths of this document, and
test/heap.c covers the scratch memory accounting.
